            <ArticleTitle>Hemoptysis caused by pulmonary sequestration in perforated appendicitis: A rare case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>569-571</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5505/tjtes.2016.99542</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pulmonary sequestration is a rare and usually asymptomatic congenital anomaly. Optimal management of this condition is still a subject of debate, including superiority of surgical resection or angiographic embolization of the aberrant arterial vessel. Presently described is rare case of a 51-year-old male who presented with hemoptysis related to pulmonary sequestration associated with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain caused by perforated appendicitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
